@Tutorial {
  @Intro(title: "Getting started") {
    Six steps, in the order the runs read them. Each names a command the dispatch registers, a flag
    that command's signature declares, or a name the configuration reads, which is what the check half
    reads the page against.

    The steps run on a console: two of them read their values from the terminal device, so they do not
    run from a unit or from a pipe.
  }

  @Section(title: "The run") {
    The first three steps write rows, the fourth reads them, and the last is the run that keeps
    running.

    @Steps {
      @Step {
        Name the data the runs read with the value the example writes: `DATABASE_URL` as
        `/var/lib/rivulet/rivulet.db`. Only this name is required, and a name the configuration does
        not read answers nothing.
      }

      @Step {
        Apply the schema the migration declares with `rivulet migrate`, which answers nothing when the
        schema is already there.
      }

      @Step {
        Create the account with `rivulet create-admin`, then the credential pair the clients log in
        with through `rivulet integration-credentials`. The account command writes the hash alone, so
        a login answers 401 until the credential command has run.
      }

      @Step {
        Add a feed with `rivulet add https://example.com/feed.xml --user alice`. The command writes the
        row and fetches nothing, so the first round is the cadence loop's own.
      }

      @Step {
        Read the counts back with `rivulet status --json`, then run the part that keeps running with
        `rivulet serve` on the port the address value names. A deployment names this run in a service
        file, and the signal that file names is a signal the process handler has a case for.
      }

      @Step {
        Take the copy before the artifact changes with `rivulet backup --verify /var/backups/rivulet.db`,
        which opens the copy the run wrote through its own connection and reports the problems it finds.
      }
    }
  }
}
